data/bench_stencil_retracted.jsonl — quarantined rows, NOT an analysis input
=============================================================================

What these rows are
--------------------
22 rows removed from the live ledger `data/bench_stencil.jsonl` by commit
`14d6920` ("bench: retract F3 artefact — remove gate-corrupted and duplicate
rows"). Every row here is a `*-active-fft` variant with `precond_count == 1`
while its own `iterations` field is 22–272 — i.e. the run's own record shows
the FFT `|k|` preconditioner was applied exactly once and the remaining
iterations ran unpreconditioned. That is the fingerprint of the
`precond_cost_gate` bug on this branch: a runtime "cost gate"
(`SolveOptions::precond_cost_gate`, default 1.5 at the time) took one
wall-clock timing sample after the first preconditioner apply and, on the
active-set restricted path (masked matvec = 1-2% of a full matvec), fired on
every `fft`-arm run, silently disabling the preconditioner from iteration 2
onward. The rows were real solver runs, not corrupted data files — the
corruption is in what they measure (an accidentally-unpreconditioned `fft`
arm), not in how they were recorded.

Why they are kept, not deleted
-------------------------------
`bench/README.md` documents the live ledger as append-only, specifically so
a reported number can be defended or refuted later from the raw
measurements. Deleting these rows outright (as `14d6920` did) removes the
only direct evidence that the cost gate fired on every `fft`-arm run at the
default workload — the most valuable lesson on this branch. They are moved
here, out of the analysis input, rather than restored to the live ledger,
because they do NOT measure what their `variant` name claims (a
preconditioned solve) and must never be picked up by `bench/analyze.py` or
any other tool that treats `data/bench_stencil.jsonl` as ground truth.

Provenance
----------
- Removed from the live ledger by commit `14d6920`
  ("bench: retract F3 artefact — remove gate-corrupted and duplicate rows").
- Recovered by diffing `git show 14d6920^:data/bench_stencil.jsonl` against
  `git show 14d6920:data/bench_stencil.jsonl` (43 rows removed in total by
  that commit).
- IMPORTANT: only 22 of those 43 removed rows fit the gate-corruption
  fingerprint (`*-active-fft` variant, `precond_count == 1`). The other 21
  are `*-active-stencil` rows with `precond_count == iterations` (i.e. NOT
  gate-corrupted) — legitimate but superseded/duplicate reps of the same
  (workload, Ns, variant, rep) key, removed in the same commit for the
  separate "duplicate rows" reason its own commit message names. Those 21
  are NOT included here: a duplicate rep is evidence of nothing, and
  resurrecting it would misrepresent this file's purpose. This file holds
  only the 22 rows that carry the actual gate-corruption fingerprint.
- A further, unrelated single duplicate row (`h2-f64-active-stencil`,
  Ns=16384) was removed from the live ledger by commit `aa2a1d2`
  ("bench: dedup duplicate Ns=16384 f64-stencil row"). That row is a true
  duplicate, is not gate-corrupted, and is correctly left deleted — it is
  not reproduced here either.

What NOT to do with this file
------------------------------
- Do not feed it to `bench/analyze.py` or add it to any `--ledger` argument.
- Do not use it to compute any wall-time or iteration-count ratio; the
  numbers it contains are, by construction, measuring an unintentionally
  unpreconditioned solve mislabeled as preconditioned.
- Do not merge it back into `data/bench_stencil.jsonl`.

See `doc/bench/2026-09-09-stencil-preconditioner.md` (the correction banner
at the top, and the R11 correction in the "Promotion verdict" section) for
the full narrative.
